###### Abstract

In this supplemental material, we give more details on the DMRG calculations and the derivations for parts of the main text.

## I DMRG results for the spin structure factor

The DMRG [1; 2] simulations have been performed on the geometry of a rectangular cylinder, with periodic boundary conditions in the \(y\) direction and open boundaries in the \(x\) direction. We study the system with \(L_{y}\) up to \(8\). By keeping up to \(4000\) SU(2) DMRG states, our calculations have the largest truncation errors around \(10^{-5}\) to show high accuracy. We calculate the spin-spin (\(\mathbf{S}_{i}\cdot\mathbf{S}_{j}\)) and quadrupolar-quadrupolar (\(\mathbf{Q}_{i}\cdot\mathbf{Q}_{j}\)) correlation functions, where \(\mathbf{Q}_{i}\) is the quadrupolar operator [33] and the quadrupolar term can be reexpressed as \(\mathbf{Q}_{i}\cdot\mathbf{Q}_{j}=2(\mathbf{S}_{i}\cdot\mathbf{S}_{j})^{2}+ \mathbf{S}_{i}\cdot\mathbf{S}_{j}-8/3\). We perform Fourier transformation for the correlation functions to obtain the spin and quadrupolar structure factors as

\[m_{\mathbf{S}}^{2}(\mathbf{q})=\frac{1}{N_{s}^{2}}\sum_{i,j}\langle\mathbf{S}_{i} \cdot\mathbf{S}_{j}\rangle e^{i\mathbf{q}\cdot(\mathbf{r}_{i}-\mathbf{r}_{j})}\] (S1)

and

\[m_{\mathbf{S}}^{2}(\mathbf{q})=\frac{1}{N_{s}^{2}}\sum_{i,j}\langle\mathbf{Q}_{i} \cdot\mathbf{Q}_{j}\rangle e^{i\mathbf{q}\cdot(\mathbf{r}_{i}-\mathbf{r}_{j})},\] (S2)

where the sites \(i,j\) are chosen over the middle \(N_{s}=L_{y}\times L_{y}\) sites in order to avoid the effects of open edge [34]. Fig. S1 shows the spin structure factor \(m_{\mathbf{S}}^{2}\) with \(J_{1}=1,K_{1}=1.2,K_{2}=-0.3\) on the \(L_{y}=8\) cylinder, which has weak peaks at momenta \((\pi,0)\) and \((0,\pi)\). The quadrupolar structure factor \(m_{\mathbf{Q}}^{2}\) has been shown in the main text.

## II 3-flavor electron representations of \(S=1\) spin and quadrupole

For conduction electrons, we consider 3-flavor electrons with flavor index \(\alpha=x,y,z\). The three flavor of fermions can be transformed to each other by SU(3) symmetry. For each lattice site, the total electron density is one, which means that each flavor of electron is 1/3-filled

\[\left\langle\psi_{i\alpha}^{\dagger}\psi_{i\alpha}\right\rangle=\frac{1}{3}\] (S3)

The spin and quadrupole operators can be written in the 3-flavor electrons as,

\[s_{c,i}^{\alpha}=-i\epsilon_{\alpha\beta\gamma}\psi_{i\beta}^{ \dagger}\psi_{i\gamma},\] (S4) \[q_{c,i}^{x^{2}-y^{2}}=-\psi_{ix}^{\dagger}\psi_{ix}+\psi_{iy}^{ \dagger}\psi_{iy},\] (S5) \[q_{c,i}^{3x^{2}-r^{2}}=(\psi_{ix}^{\dagger}\psi_{ix}+\psi_{iy}^{ \dagger}\psi_{iy}-2\psi_{iz}^{\dagger}\psi_{iz})/\sqrt{3},\] (S6) \[q_{c,i}^{x\varphi}=-\psi_{ix}^{\dagger}\psi_{iy}-\psi_{iy}^{ \dagger}\psi_{ix},\] (S7) \[q_{c,i}^{yz}=-\psi_{iy}^{\dagger}\psi_{iz}-\psi_{iz}^{\dagger} \psi_{iy},\] (S8) \[q_{c,i}^{zx}=-\psi_{iz}^{\dagger}\psi_{ix}-\psi_{ix}^{\dagger} \psi_{iz}.\] (S9)

The spin and quadrupolar operators can also be transformed to each other under the SU(3) rotations.

## Continuum theory at SU(3) point

Let's focus first on the SU(3) point, where \(J_{n}=K_{n}\). We consider the time-reversal invariance basis

\[|x\rangle=i\frac{|1\rangle-|\bar{1}\rangle}{\sqrt{2}},\ \ |y\rangle=\frac{|1 \rangle+|\bar{1}\rangle}{\sqrt{2}},\ \ |z\rangle=-i|0\rangle,\] (S10)

where \(|S^{z}=1\rangle\equiv|1\rangle\) and etc. A general wave function at a site \(j\) can be written in the form

\[|{\bf d}_{j}\rangle=d_{j}^{x}|x\rangle+d_{j}^{y}|y\rangle+d_{j}^{z}|z\rangle,\] (S11)

where we can introduce the vector notation as

\[{\bf d}_{j}=(d_{j}^{x},\ d_{j}^{y},\ d_{j}^{z})\] (S12)

is a 3 vector of complex numbers. Separating the real and imaginary parts of \({\bf d}_{j}\) gives

\[{\bf d}_{j}={\bf u}_{j}+i{\bf v}_{j}.\] (S13)

Requiring the wave functions to be normalizd gives the constraints,

\[{\bf d}_{j}\cdot\bar{\bf d}_{j}=1\rightarrow{\bf u}_{j}^{2}+{\bf v}_{j}^{2}=1.\] (S14)

The overall phase can be fixed by requiring

\[{\bf d}_{j}^{2}=\bar{\bf d}_{j}^{2}\rightarrow{\bf u}_{j}\cdot{\bf v}_{j}=0.\] (S15)

Within the spin-coherent state framework, we can obtain

\[{\bf S}_{j}=2{\bf u}_{j}\times{\bf v}_{j},\] (S16)

and in terms of the components of the \({\bf d}\) we can obtain

\[S^{\alpha}=-i\epsilon^{\alpha\beta\gamma}\bar{d}^{\beta}d^{ \gamma},\] (S17) \[Q^{x^{2}-y^{2}}=-|d^{x}|^{2}+|d^{y}|^{2},\] (S18) \[Q^{3z^{2}-r^{2}}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{3}}\left[|d^{x}|^{2}+|d^{y}|^{2} -2|d^{z}|^{2}\right],\] (S19) \[Q^{xy}=-\bar{d}^{x}d^{y}-\bar{d}^{y}d^{x},\] (S20) \[Q^{yz}=-\bar{d}^{y}d^{z}-\bar{d}^{z}d^{y},\] (S21) \[Q^{zx}=-\bar{d}^{z}d^{x}-\bar{d}^{x}d^{z}.\] (S22)The Hamiltonian can be re-expressed as

\[H_{S}= \sum_{\langle ij\rangle}\left[J_{1}|\mathbf{d}_{i}\cdot\bar{ \mathbf{d}}_{j}|^{2}+(K_{1}-J_{1})|\mathbf{d}_{i}\cdot\mathbf{d}_{j}|^{2}+K_{1 }\right]+\sum_{\langle\langle ij\rangle\rangle}\left[J_{2}|\mathbf{d}_{i}\cdot \bar{\mathbf{d}}_{j}|^{2}+(K_{2}-J_{2})|\mathbf{d}_{i}\cdot\mathbf{d}_{j}|^{2}+ K_{2}\right]\] \[\equiv \sum_{\langle ij\rangle}\left[J_{1}|\mathbf{d}_{i}\cdot\bar{ \mathbf{d}}_{j}|^{2}+J_{1}^{\prime}|\mathbf{d}_{i}\cdot\mathbf{d}_{j}|^{2}+K_{ 1}\right]+\sum_{\langle\langle ij\rangle\rangle}\left[J_{2}|\mathbf{d}_{i} \cdot\bar{\mathbf{d}}_{j}|^{2}+J_{2}^{\prime}|\mathbf{d}_{i}\cdot\mathbf{d}_{ j}|^{2}+K_{2}\right],\]

where we defined \(K_{n}-J_{n}\equiv J_{n}^{\prime}\) which break the SU(3) symmetry and from now on we will ignore the constants. At SU(3) point, we can see that that Hamiltonian becomes

\[H_{S}^{SU(3)}=\sum_{\langle ij\rangle}J_{1}|\mathbf{d}_{i}\cdot\bar{\mathbf{d }}_{j}|^{2}+\sum_{\langle\langle ij\rangle\rangle}J_{2}|\mathbf{d}_{i}\cdot \bar{\mathbf{d}}_{j}|^{2}.\] (S23)

For obtaining the non-linear sigma model (NLsM) description, we can follow the approaches detailed by A. Smerald _et al._[5] One choice for the ground state of such a \((\pi,\pi)\)-AFQ is

\[\mathbf{d}_{A}^{gs}=\begin{pmatrix}1\\ 0\\ 0\end{pmatrix},\ \mathbf{d}_{B}^{gs}=\begin{pmatrix}0\\ 1\\ 0\end{pmatrix}.\] (S24)

The Hamiltonian, Eq. (S23), is invariant under the global rotation \(\mathbf{d}\rightarrow\mathbf{U}\mathbf{d}\), provided that \(\mathbf{U}^{-1}=\mathbf{U}^{\dagger}\). In general, there should be 6 distinct generators for SU(3), however, for the present \((\pi,\pi)\)-AFQ phase with only two directors, only 4 out of 6 are needed for a complete description of the global rotations which maintain the energy of the ground state. Fig. S2 illustrate the effects of all possible rotations, some of which preserve the energy while some increase the energy. Let's first study the global rotations.

The natural choices of the matrices at SU(3) are Gell-Mann matrices, which can be used to describe the rotation of the ground state configuration. Ignoring the diagonal Gell-Mann matrices, we are left with 6 matrices. However, only 4 are needed for a complete description of the global rotations for the \((\pi,\pi)\)-AFQ with only two mutually orthogonal directors. The 4 Gell-Mann matrices I choose are,

\[\lambda_{1}=\begin{pmatrix}0&-i&0\\ i&0&0\\ 0&0&0\end{pmatrix}\ \ \lambda_{2}=\begin{pmatrix}0&0&i\\ 0&0&0\\ -i&0&0\end{pmatrix}\ \ \ \ \lambda_{3}=\begin{pmatrix}0&0&0\\ 0&0&-i\\ 0&i&0\end{pmatrix}\ \ \lambda_{4}=\begin{pmatrix}0&1&0\\ 1&0&0\\ 0&0&0\end{pmatrix}.\] (S25)

The global rotation in complex space can be written as

\[\mathbf{U}(\phi)\equiv e^{i\sum_{j=1}^{4}\lambda_{j}\phi_{j}}.\] (S26)

Fig. S2 (b) gives an illustration of the global rotation around real \(\hat{z}\)-axis using \(\mathbf{U}(\phi_{1},0,0,0)\).

On the other hand, we also need to consider the rotations involving canting of the directors ground state configurations, which increases the energy. The canting fields I choose are

\[\mu_{1}=\begin{pmatrix}0&-i&0\\ -i&0&0\\ 0&0&0\end{pmatrix}\ \ \mu_{2}=\begin{pmatrix}0&0&-1\\ 0&0&0\\ 1&0&0\end{pmatrix}\ \ \mu_{3}=\begin{pmatrix}0&0&0\\ 0&0&1\\ 0&-1&0\end{pmatrix}\ \ \mu_{4}=\begin{pmatrix}0&1&0\\ -1&0&0\\ 0&0&0\end{pmatrix}.\] (S27)

The general configuration of \(\mathbf{d}_{A}\) and \(\mathbf{d}_{B}\) can be obtained by operating the general rotation on the chosen ground state configurations \(\mathbf{d}_{A}^{gs}\) and \(\mathbf{d}_{B}^{gs}\) described above,

\[\mathbf{D}(\phi,\ell,v)=e^{i\sum_{p=1}^{4}\lambda_{p}\phi_{p}+i\mu_{1}\ell_{1} ^{*}+i\mu_{2}v_{A}+i\mu_{3}v_{B}+i\mu_{4}\ell_{2}^{*}},\] (S28)

where

\[\mathbf{d}_{A}=\mathbf{D}(\phi,\ell,v)\mathbf{d}_{A}^{gs},\ \ \mathbf{d}_{B}= \mathbf{D}(\phi,\ell,v)\mathbf{d}_{B}^{gs}.\] (S29)

Expanding the canting terms up to quadratic order, we can approximate

\[\mathbf{d}_{A} \simeq\mathbf{U}(\phi)\begin{pmatrix}1-\frac{1}{2}\left[(\ell_{1 }^{z})^{2}+(\ell_{2}^{z})^{2}+v_{A}^{2}\right]\\ \ell_{1}^{z}-i\ell_{2}^{z}\\ iv_{A}\end{pmatrix}\] (S30) \[\mathbf{d}_{B} \simeq\mathbf{U}(\phi)\begin{pmatrix}\ell_{1}^{z}+i\ell_{2}^{z} \\ 1-\frac{1}{2}\left[(\ell_{1}^{z})^{2}+(\ell_{2}^{z})^{2}+v_{B}^{2}\right]\\ -iv_{B}\end{pmatrix}\] (S31)

Introducing \(\ell^{z}\equiv\ell_{1}^{z}+i\ell_{2}^{z}\) and reparametrizing

\[\mathbf{U}=\begin{pmatrix}n_{A}^{x}&n_{B}^{x}&n_{C}^{x}\\ n_{A}^{y}&n_{B}^{y}&n_{C}^{y}\\ n_{A}^{z}&n_{B}^{z}&n_{C}^{z}\end{pmatrix}\] (S32)

where the vectors \(\mathbf{n}_{a}\) inherit the constraints of \(\mathbf{d}\) with

\[\mathbf{n}_{a}\cdot\bar{\mathbf{n}}_{b\neq a}=0,\ \ \mathbf{n}_{a}^{2}= \mathbf{n}_{a}^{2}\] (S33) \[\mathbf{n}_{a}\cdot\bar{\mathbf{n}}_{a}=1,\] (S34)

where the vector \(\mathbf{n}_{c}\equiv\bar{\mathbf{n}}_{A}\times\bar{\mathbf{n}}_{B}\) is introduced as a convenient piece of book-keeping in the present \((\pi,\pi)\)-AFQ (two sub lattice order), and is not an independent degrees of freedom.

Going to continuum limit involves the assumption that physically interesting variation takes place on a length scale much larger than the lattice constant \(a\equiv 1\) and so gradients within the placate are small. In addition, the continuum field theory should describe the dynamics of both the broken symmetry state and the nearby paramagnetic region, in which the order parameter is assumed to be locally robust but slowly varying over macroscopic length sales. It is therefore necessary to allow the fields to fluctuate in space and time. From now on, all the parameters are allowed to fluctuate in space-time domain, \((\mathbf{r},\tau)\).

The partition function is

\[\mathcal{Z}_{\square}^{SU(3)}=\int D[\mathbf{d}]e^{-S_{\square}^{SU(3)}[ \mathbf{d}]},\] (S35)

where the action includes \(S_{\square}^{SU(3)}=S_{kinetic}+S_{Hs}\). Let's focus on the Hamiltonian term first.

### Continuum theory for Hamiltonian terms

The action for the Hamiltonian term is

\[S_{Hs} =\int_{0}^{\beta}d\tau H_{S}^{SU(3)}\] \[=\frac{1}{2}\int_{0}^{\beta}d\tau\int d^{2}\mathbf{r}H_{S,clus}^{ SU(3)},\] (S36)where we focus on one cluster instead of a single site and the \(2\) in the denominator is the effective cluster area. Within the cluster picture, we can write down the Hamiltonian term in terms of \(\mathbf{d}_{j}\). Then we can perform a gradient expansion,

\[\mathbf{d}_{j}(\mathbf{r}+\epsilon_{i},\tau)\simeq\mathbf{d}_{j}( \mathbf{r},\tau)+(\epsilon_{i}\cdot\nabla)\mathbf{d}_{j}(\mathbf{r},\tau)+ \frac{1}{2}(\epsilon_{i}\cdot\nabla)^{2}\mathbf{d}_{j}(\mathbf{r},\tau),\]

to give a continuum theory of the Hamiltonian term. For a square cluster with 4 sites, let's explicitly write down the Hamiltonian terms below.

\[H_{S,clus}^{SU(3)}=H_{J_{1},clus}^{SU(3)}+H_{J_{2},clus}^{SU(3)},\]

where

\[H_{J_{1},clus}^{SU(3)}= -J_{1}\bigg{[}\bigg{|}\mathbf{d}_{A}(\mathbf{r}+(-\frac{1}{2},- \frac{1}{2}),\tau)\cdot\bar{\mathbf{d}}_{B}(\mathbf{r}+(\frac{1}{2},-\frac{1} {2}),\tau)\bigg{|}^{2}+\bigg{|}\mathbf{d}_{A}(\mathbf{r}+(-\frac{1}{2},-\frac {1}{2}),\tau)\cdot\bar{\mathbf{d}}_{A}(\mathbf{r}+(-\frac{1}{2},\frac{1}{2}), \tau)\bigg{|}^{2}\] \[H_{J_{2},clus}^{SU(3)}= J_{2}\bigg{[}\bigg{|}\mathbf{d}_{A}(\mathbf{r}+(-\frac{1}{2},- \frac{1}{2}),\tau)\cdot\bar{\mathbf{d}}_{A}(\mathbf{r}+(\frac{1}{2},\frac{1}{2 }),\tau)\bigg{|}^{2}+\bigg{|}\mathbf{d}_{B}(\mathbf{r}+(\frac{1}{2},-\frac{1}{ 2}),\tau)\cdot\bar{\mathbf{d}}_{A}(\mathbf{r}+(-\frac{1}{2},\frac{1}{2}),\tau) \bigg{|}^{2}\] \[+\bigg{|}\mathbf{d}_{A}(\mathbf{r}+(\frac{1}{2},\frac{1}{2}),\tau )\cdot\bar{\mathbf{d}}_{A}(\mathbf{r}+(-\frac{1}{2},\frac{3}{2}),\tau)\bigg{|} ^{2}+\bigg{|}\mathbf{d}_{B}(\mathbf{r}+(-\frac{1}{2},\frac{1}{2}),\tau)\cdot \bar{\mathbf{d}}_{A}(\mathbf{r}+(\frac{1}{2},\frac{3}{2}),\tau)\bigg{|}^{2}\] (S39)

We now perform gradient expansion for each term

1. \(J_{1}\) terms: \[4\left|\mathbf{d}_{A}\cdot\bar{\mathbf{d}}_{B}\right|^{2}+2\sum_{ \lambda=x,y}\left|\boldsymbol{d}_{A}\cdot\partial_{\lambda}\bar{\boldsymbol{d }}_{B}\right|^{2}.\] (S40)
2. \(J_{2}\) terms: \[-2\sum_{\lambda=x,y}\left[\left|\partial_{\lambda}\boldsymbol{d}_{A}\right|^{ 2}+\left|\partial_{\lambda}\boldsymbol{d}_{B}\right|^{2}-2\left|\boldsymbol{d }_{A}\partial_{\lambda}\bar{\boldsymbol{d}}_{A}\right|^{2}-\left|\boldsymbol{d }_{B}\partial_{\lambda}\bar{\boldsymbol{d}}_{B}\right|^{2}\right].\] (S41)

Combining all terms leads and using Eqs. (S30)-(S31),

\[\left|\mathbf{d}_{A}\cdot\bar{\mathbf{d}}_{B}\right|^{2}\simeq 4 \left[(\ell_{1}^{z})^{2}+(\ell_{2}^{z})^{2}\right],\] (S42) \[\left|\mathbf{d}_{A}\cdot\partial_{\lambda=x,y}\bar{\mathbf{d}} _{B}\right|^{2}\simeq\left|\mathbf{n}_{A}\cdot\partial_{\lambda}\bar{\mathbf{ n}}_{B}\right|^{2},\] (S43) \[\mathbf{d}_{A}\cdot\bar{\mathbf{d}}_{A}\simeq\mathbf{d}_{B}\cdot \bar{\mathbf{d}}_{B}\simeq 1,\] (S44)

we obtain that

\[H_{S,clus}^{SU(3)}\simeq 16J_{1}\left[\left(\ell_{1}^{z}\right)^{2}+(\ell_{2}^{z})^{2} \right]+2J_{1}\sum_{\lambda=x,y}\left|\boldsymbol{n}_{A}\partial_{x}\bar{ \boldsymbol{n}}_{B}\right|^{2}-2J_{2}\sum_{\lambda=x,y}\bigg{[}\left|\partial_ {\lambda}\boldsymbol{n}_{A}\right|^{2}+\left|\partial_{\lambda}\boldsymbol{n} _{B}\right|^{2}-\left|\boldsymbol{n}_{A}\partial_{\lambda}\boldsymbol{n}_{A} \right|^{2}-\left|\boldsymbol{n}_{B}\partial_{\lambda}\bar{\boldsymbol{n}}_{B} \right|^{2}\bigg{]},\] (S45)

where we explicitly ignore constant terms.

### Continuum theory for the kinetic terms

The action for the kinetic terms is quantum-mechanical in origin and is

\[S_{kin}\simeq\int_{0}^{\beta}\frac{1}{2}\int d^{2}\mathbf{r}\sum_{a=A,B}\bar{ \mathbf{d}}_{a}\partial_{\tau}\mathbf{d}_{a}=\frac{1}{2}\int_{0}^{\beta}\int d ^{2}\mathbf{r}\left[\bar{\mathbf{d}}_{A}\partial_{\tau}\mathbf{d}_{A}+\bar{ \mathbf{d}}_{B}\partial_{\tau}\mathbf{d}_{B}\right].\] (S46)

Using again Eqs. (S30)-(S31) leads to the Lagrangian for the kinetic term as \[\mathcal{L}_{kin,clus}\simeq \bar{\mathbf{n}}_{A}\partial_{r}\mathbf{n}_{A}+\bar{\mathbf{n}}_{B} \partial_{r}\mathbf{n}_{B}+2\ell_{1}^{z}\left(\bar{\mathbf{n}}_{A}\partial_{r} \mathbf{n}_{B}-\mathbf{n}_{A}\partial_{r}\bar{\mathbf{n}}_{B}\right)-2i\ell_{ 2}^{z}\left(\bar{\mathbf{n}}_{A}\partial_{r}\mathbf{n}_{B}+\mathbf{n}_{A} \partial_{r}\bar{\mathbf{n}}_{B}\right)\] (S47) \[-iv_{A}\left(\bar{\mathbf{n}}_{e}\partial_{r}\mathbf{n}_{A}+ \mathbf{n}_{c}\partial_{r}\bar{\mathbf{n}}_{A}\right)-iv_{B}\left(\bar{ \mathbf{n}}_{B}\partial_{r}\mathbf{n}_{c}+\mathbf{n}_{B}\partial_{r}\bar{ \mathbf{n}}_{c}\right).\]

Therefore, the Lagrangian for continuum theory is \(\mathcal{L}_{S}^{NLsM}=\mathcal{L}_{kin,clus}+\mathcal{L}_{S,clus}^{SU(3)}\), Eq. (S45) + Eq. (S47).

### Away from SU(3) point

We now introduce SU(3) breaking terms to break the SU(3) down to SU(2). In principle, if we focus on the AFQ phase, the SU(2) symmetry will guarantee there are 3 gapless Goldstone modes associated with the quadrupole wave fluctuations. The SU(3)-breaking terms added are

\[H^{\prime}=J_{1}^{\prime}\sum_{\langle ij\rangle}\left(\vec{S}_{i}\cdot\vec{ S}_{j}\right)^{2}+J_{2}^{\prime}\sum_{\langle\langle ij\rangle\rangle}\left( \vec{S}_{i}\cdot\vec{S}_{j}\right)^{2}.\] (S48)

In terms of \(\mathbf{d}\) framework,

\[H^{\prime}= J_{1}^{\prime}\sum_{\langle ij\rangle}\left|\mathbf{d}_{i}\cdot \mathbf{d}_{j}\right|^{2}+J_{2}^{\prime}\sum_{\langle\langle ij\rangle\rangle }\left|\mathbf{d}_{i}\cdot\mathbf{d}_{j}\right|^{2},\]

where we ignore the constant terms. We again focus on a cluster and perform gradient expansion. Let's write down each contributions separately.

1. \(J_{1}^{\prime}\) terms: \[4\left|\mathbf{d}_{A}\cdot\mathbf{d}_{B}\right|^{2}+2\sum_{\lambda=x,y}\left| \mathbf{d}_{A}\cdot\partial_{\lambda}\mathbf{d}_{B}\right|^{2}.\] (S49)
2. \(J_{2}^{\prime}\) terms: \[\mathbf{d}_{A}^{2}\vec{\mathbf{d}_{A}}^{2}+\mathbf{d}_{B}^{2}\vec{\mathbf{d}_{B}}^{2}-\sum_{ \lambda=x,y}\left[\left(\partial_{\lambda}\mathbf{d}_{A}\right)^{2}+\left( \partial_{\lambda}\vec{\mathbf{d}}_{A}\right)^{2}+\left(\partial_{\lambda}\mathbf{d}_ {B}\right)^{2}+\left(\partial_{\lambda}\vec{\mathbf{d}}_{B}\right)^{2}\right].\] (S50)

We then use Eqs. (S30)-(S31) simplify the results above in terms of the parametrization fields \(\mathbf{n}_{a}\) and the canting fields \(\ell_{1}^{z},\ell_{2}^{z},v_{A},v_{B}\). The only new assumption we need to use is that in the AFQ phase the parametrization fields \(\mathbf{n}_{a}\) inherit the constraints of the original vector \(\mathbf{d}\). In AFQ, \(\mathbf{d}\) is purely real or purely imaginary. Without loss of generality, we assume it is purely real. The additional constraints are

\[\mathbf{n}_{a}\cdot\bar{\mathbf{n}}_{a}=1\rightarrow\mathbf{n}_{a }^{2}\simeq 1,\] (S51) \[\mathbf{n}_{a}\cdot\bar{\mathbf{n}}_{b\neq a}=0\rightarrow\mathbf{ n}_{a}\cdot\mathbf{n}_{b\neq a}\ll 1.\] (S52)

We can then obtain approximately,

\[\mathbf{d}_{A}^{2}\simeq 1-2\left(\ell_{2}^{z}\right)^{2}-2v_{A}^{2},\] (S53) \[\left|\mathbf{d}_{A}\cdot\mathbf{d}_{A}\right|^{2}=\mathbf{d}_{A}^{2 }\vec{\mathbf{d}_{A}}^{2}\simeq 1-4\left(\ell_{2}^{z}\right)^{2}-4v_{A}^{2},\] (S54) \[\left|\mathbf{d}_{B}\cdot\mathbf{d}_{B}\right|^{2}=\mathbf{d}_{B}^{2 }\vec{\mathbf{d}_{B}}^{2}\simeq 1-4\left(\ell_{2}^{z}\right)^{2}-4v_{B}^{2},\] (S55)

where we used the approximation \(\mathbf{n}_{A}^{2}\bar{\mathbf{n}}_{A}^{2}\simeq 1\simeq\mathbf{n}_{B}^{2}\bar{\mathbf{n}}_{B}^{2}\). In the end, we can conclude that SU(3) breaking Hamiltonian i (ignoring constants)

\[H_{clus}^{\prime}\simeq J_{1}^{\prime}\left[4\left|\mathbf{n}_{A}\cdot\mathbf{n}_{B}\right|^{2}+16 \left(\ell_{1}^{z}\right)^{2}+2\sum_{\lambda}\left|\mathbf{n}_{A}\partial_{\lambda }\mathbf{n}_{B}\right|^{2}\right]\] \[-J_{2}^{\prime}\left[16\left(\ell_{2}^{z}\right)^{2}+8\left(v_{A }^{2}+v_{B}^{2}\right)+\sum_{\lambda}\left(\left(\partial_{\lambda}\mathbf{n}_{A} \right)^{2}+\left(\partial_{\lambda}\bar{\mathbf{n}}_{A}\right)^{2}+\left(\partial _{\lambda}\mathbf{n}_{B}\right)^{2}+\left(\partial_{\lambda}\bar{\mathbf{n}}_{B}\right) ^{2}\right)\right].\] (S56)For obtaining the NLsM for the \((\pi,\pi)\)-AFQ, we need to combine Eq. (S45), Eq. (S47), and Eq. (S56) and integrate out the canting fields within the steepest-decent approximation. Solving the differential equations,

\[\frac{\delta\mathcal{L}}{\delta\varepsilon^{z}}=0;\quad\frac{ \delta\mathcal{L}}{\delta\varepsilon^{z}}=0,\] (S57) \[\frac{\delta\mathcal{L}}{\delta\nu_{A}}=0;\quad\frac{\delta \mathcal{L}}{\delta\varepsilon_{B}}=0.\]

We obtain

\[\ell_{1}^{z} =-\frac{\bar{n}_{A}\partial_{\tau}n_{B}-n_{A}\partial_{\tau}\bar{ n}_{B}}{16(J_{1}+J_{1}^{\prime})},\] (S58) \[\ell_{2}^{z} =\frac{i(\bar{n}_{A}\partial_{\tau}n_{B}+n_{A}\partial_{\tau}\bar{ n}_{B})}{16(J_{1}-J_{2}^{\prime})},\] (S59) \[v_{A} =-\frac{i(\bar{n}_{C}\partial_{\tau}n_{A}+n_{C}\partial_{\tau} \bar{n}_{A})}{16J_{2}^{\prime}},\] (S60) \[v_{B} =-\frac{i(\bar{n}_{B}\partial_{\tau}n_{C}+n_{B}\partial_{\tau} \bar{n}_{C})}{16J_{2}^{\prime}}.\] (S61)

Plugging the solutions back to the Lagrangian, we obtain the \(\mathcal{L}_{NLsM,plus}\)

\[\mathcal{L}_{NLsM,clus}= \bar{n}_{A}\partial_{\tau}n_{A}+\bar{n}_{B}\partial_{\tau}n_{B}-\] \[-\frac{\left[\bar{n}_{A}\partial_{\tau}n_{B}-n_{A}\partial_{\tau }\bar{n}_{B}\right]^{2}}{16(J_{1}+J_{1}^{\prime})}+\frac{\left[\bar{n}_{A} \partial_{\tau}n_{B}+n_{A}\partial_{\tau}\bar{n}_{B}\right]^{2}}{16(J_{1}-J_{ 2}^{\prime})}-\frac{\left[\bar{n}_{C}\partial_{\tau}n_{A}+n_{C}\partial_{\tau }\bar{n}_{A}\right]}{32J_{2}^{\prime}}-\frac{\left[\bar{n}_{B}\partial_{\tau} n_{C}+n_{B}\partial_{\tau}\bar{n}_{C}\right]^{2}}{32J_{2}^{\prime}}+\] \[+2J_{1}\sum_{\lambda=x,y}\left|n_{A}\partial_{\lambda}\bar{n}_{B }\right|^{2}+2J_{1}^{\prime}\sum_{\lambda}\left|n_{A}\partial_{\lambda}n_{B} \right|^{2}-2J_{2}\sum_{\lambda}\left[\left|\partial_{\lambda}n_{A}\right|^{2} +\left|\partial_{\lambda}n_{B}\right|^{2}-\left|\bar{n}_{A}\partial_{\lambda} n_{A}\right|^{2}-\left|\bar{n}_{B}\partial_{\lambda}n_{B}\right|^{2}\right]+\] \[-J_{2}^{\prime}\sum_{\lambda}\left[\left(\partial_{\lambda}n_{A} \right)^{2}+\left(\partial_{\lambda}\bar{n}_{A}\right)^{2}+\left(\partial_{ \lambda}n_{B}\right)^{2}+\left(\partial_{\lambda}\bar{n}_{B}\right)^{2}\right] +4J_{1}^{\prime}\left|n_{A}\cdot n_{B}\right|^{2}.\] (S62)

We now derive the linearized action by re-expressing \(\mathbf{n}_{a}\) in terms of bosonic fields \(\phi_{1,2,3,4}\), with

\[n_{A}\simeq\begin{pmatrix}1\\ -\phi_{1}+i\phi_{4}\\ \phi_{2}\end{pmatrix};\ n_{B}\simeq\begin{pmatrix}\phi_{1}+i\phi_{4}\\ 1\\ -\phi_{3}\end{pmatrix};\ n_{C}\simeq\begin{pmatrix}-\phi_{2}\\ \phi_{3}\\ 1\end{pmatrix},\]

the linearized harmonic Lagrangian for \((\pi,\pi)\)-AFQ is

\[\mathcal{L}\simeq \frac{(\partial_{\tau}\phi_{1})^{2}}{4(J_{1}+J_{2}-K_{2})}+2(K_{1} -2K_{2})\sum_{\lambda}(\partial_{\lambda}\phi_{1})^{2}+\sum_{a=2,3}\left[ \frac{(\partial_{\tau}\phi_{a})^{2}}{8(J_{2}-K_{2})}-2K_{2}\sum_{\lambda}( \partial_{\lambda}\phi_{a})^{2}\right]+\] \[+\frac{(\partial_{\tau}\phi_{4})^{2}}{4K_{1}}+2(K_{1}+2K_{2}-4J_{ 2})\sum_{\lambda}(\partial_{\lambda}\phi_{4})^{2}+16(K_{1}-J_{1})\phi_{4}^{2}-\] \[\equiv\mathcal{L}_{S}^{NLsM}(\phi_{1},\phi_{2},\phi_{3},\phi_{4}).\] (S64)

Ignoring the terms that couple to the conduction electrons' degrees of freedom, we can see the stability for \((\pi,\pi)\)-AFQ requires positive stiffness leading to the conditions

\[K_{1}>0,\quad K_{2}<0,\] (S65) \[K_{1}-J_{1}\geq 0,\quad J_{2}-K_{2}>0,\] (S66) \[J_{1}+J_{2}-K_{2}>0,\quad K_{1}+2K_{2}-4J_{2}\geq 0.\] (S67)

If we focus on the regime where \(K_{1}>J_{1}\), we can see that \(\phi_{4}\) is always gapped due to the finite mass term and can be ignored. The effective low-energy description of the action, \(S_{(\pi,\pi)-AFQ}^{NLsM}\), is

\[S_{(\pi,\pi)-AFQ}^{NLsM}\simeq\int_{0}^{\beta}\frac{d\tau}{2}\int d^{2}r\Bigg{\{} \frac{(\partial_{\tau}\phi_{1})^{2}}{4(J_{1}+J_{2}-K_{2})}+2(K_{1}-2K_{2})\sum_ {\lambda}(\partial_{\lambda}\phi_{1})^{2}+\sum_{a=2,3}\left[\frac{(\partial_{ \tau}\phi_{a})^{2}}{8(J_{2}-K_{2})}-2K_{2}\sum_{\lambda}(\partial_{\lambda} \phi_{a})^{2}\right]\Bigg{\}}.\] (S68)

## Kondo effects in the \((\pi,\pi)\)-Afq

We now consider the Kondo effects in the \((\pi,\pi)\)-AFQ. Eqs. (S30)-(S31) give the general parameterizations for the \(\mathbf{d}\) vectors on the sublattices A and B as

\[\mathbf{d}_{A} =\mathbf{n}_{A}\left[1-\frac{1}{2}\left|\ell^{z}\right|^{2}-\frac{1}{2 }v_{A}^{2}\right]+\mathbf{n}_{B}\tilde{\ell}^{z}+\mathbf{n}_{c}\left(iv_{A}\right),\] (S69) \[\mathbf{d}_{B} =\mathbf{n}_{B}\left[1-\frac{1}{2}\left|\ell^{z}\right|^{2}-\frac{1}{2 }v_{B}^{2}\right]+\mathbf{n}_{A}\ell^{z}+\mathbf{n}_{c}\left(-iv_{B}\right).\] (S70)

In order to extract the low-energy description of the Kondo coupling, we first need to extract the low-energy continuum theory descriptions of the 8 component operator that consists of 3-component \(S^{x,y,z}\), and 5-component \(Q^{x^{2}-y^{2}},\;Q^{3z^{2}-r^{2}},\;Q^{xy},\;Q^{yz},\;Q^{zx}\). Since we have the expressions of the fluctuating \(\mathbf{d}\) vectors, we can write down the fluctuating 8-component dipole-quadrupole moment,

\[\mathcal{Q}_{A}=\begin{pmatrix}0&-2v_{A}-(n_{B}^{z}-n_{B}^{z}) \\ i\left(\ell^{z}-\tilde{\ell}^{z}\right)-i(n_{A}^{y}-\bar{n}_{A}^{y})\\ -1&\\ \frac{1}{\sqrt{3}}\\ -\left(\ell^{z}+\bar{\ell}^{z}\right)-(n_{A}^{y}+\bar{n}_{A}^{y})\\ 0&\\ -(n_{A}^{z}+\bar{n}_{A}^{z})\end{pmatrix},\;\mathcal{Q}_{B}=\begin{pmatrix}-2v _{B}-(n_{B}^{z}-\bar{n}_{B}^{z})\\ 0\\ i\left(\ell^{z}-\tilde{\ell}^{z}\right)+i(n_{B}^{x}-\bar{n}_{B}^{x})\\ \frac{1}{\sqrt{3}}\\ -\left(\ell^{z}+\bar{\ell}^{z}\right)-(n_{B}^{x}+\bar{n}_{B}^{z})\\ 0\end{pmatrix}.\] (S71)

We can then write down the low-energy descriptions of the 8-component moment as

\[\mathcal{Q}(\mathbf{r},t)\simeq\begin{pmatrix}-\begin{bmatrix}v_{B}+ \frac{i}{2}\left(n_{B}^{z}-\bar{n}_{B}^{z}\right)\\ -\begin{bmatrix}v_{A}-\frac{1}{2}\left(n_{A}^{z}-\bar{n}_{A}^{z}\right)\\ \end{bmatrix}\\ -2\ell_{2}^{z}-\frac{i}{2}\left(n_{A}^{y}-\bar{n}_{A}^{y}-n_{B}^{z}+\bar{n}_{B }^{x}\right)\\ 0\\ \frac{1}{\sqrt{3}}\\ -2\ell_{1}^{z}-\frac{1}{2}\left(n_{A}^{y}+\bar{n}_{A}^{y}+n_{B}^{x}+\bar{n}_{ B}^{x}\right)\\ -\frac{1}{2}\left(n_{B}^{z}+\bar{n}_{B}^{z}\right)\\ -\frac{1}{2}\left(n_{A}^{z}+\bar{n}_{A}^{z}\right)\end{pmatrix}+\begin{pmatrix} -\begin{bmatrix}v_{B}+\frac{i}{2}\left(n_{B}^{z}-\bar{n}_{B}^{z}\right)\\ \left[v_{A}-\frac{1}{2}\left(n_{A}^{z}-\bar{n}_{A}^{z}\right)\\ \frac{i}{2}\left(n_{A}^{y}-\bar{n}_{A}^{x}+\bar{n}_{B}^{x}-\bar{n}_{B}^{x} \right)\\ 1\\ \frac{1}{2}\left[n_{A}^{y}+\bar{n}_{A}^{y}-\left(n_{B}^{x}+\bar{n}_{B}^{x} \right)\right]\\ -\frac{1}{2}\left(n_{B}^{z}+\bar{n}_{B}^{z}\right)\\ \frac{1}{2}\left(n_{A}^{z}+\bar{n}_{A}^{z}\right)\end{pmatrix}\cos(\mathbf{K} \cdot\mathbf{r})\] (S72) \[\equiv\mathbf{Q}_{\mathbf{0}}+\mathbf{Q}_{\mathbf{M}}\cos\left(\mathbf{M}\cdot\mathbf{r} \right),\] (S73)

where we explicitly separate out the uniform part and the oscillatory part. First we note that the uniform part contains a "static" background of \(Q^{3z^{2}-r^{2}}\) that directly couple to the \(q_{c}^{3z^{2}-r^{2}}\) of the 3-flavor conduction electrons. This static background \(Q^{3z^{2}-r^{2}}\) field is only invariant under rotation between \(x\)-\(y\) plane, which, therefore, should break the SU(3) symmetry of the conduction elections down to SU(2)\(\times\)U(1), where SU(2) is spanned by the \(c_{x}\) and \(c_{y}\) conduction elections and U(1) is spanned by \(c_{z}\). We then expect that the low-energy descriptions of conduction electrons \(c_{x}\) and \(c_{y}\) should be different from that of \(c_{z}\), _i.e._, the Fermi velocities of \(c_{x}\) and \(c_{y}\) are the same (\(v_{x}=v_{y}\)) but is different from that of \(c_{z}\) (\(v_{z}\)).

Explicitly, the low-energy theory of the conduction electrons in the presence of the static quadrupolar background is

\[S_{c}\simeq\!\!\sum_{\alpha=x,y}\int d^{d}\mathbf{K}d\epsilon\psi_{ \alpha}^{\dagger}(\mathbf{K},i\epsilon)(i\epsilon-\xi_{k})\psi_{\alpha}(\mathbf{K},i \epsilon)+\int d^{d}\mathbf{K}^{\prime}d\epsilon^{\prime}\psi_{z}^{\dagger}(\mathbf{K} ^{\prime},i\epsilon^{\prime})(i\epsilon^{\prime}-\tilde{\xi}_{K^{\prime}})\psi_ {z}(\mathbf{K}^{\prime},i\epsilon^{\prime}),\] (S74)

where \(\xi_{K}=v_{F}(K-K_{F})\), and \(\tilde{\xi}_{\mu}=\tilde{v}_{F}(K^{\prime}-K_{F}^{\prime})\), where \(k_{F}\) and \(k_{F}^{\prime}\) are generically different. We want to remark that due to the band splitting between the \(x\), \(y\)- and \(z\)- fermions, the spin dipolar and quadrupolar degrees of freedom consisting of \(\psi_{x/y}\) and \(\psi_{z}\) fermions, _i. e._, \(s_{c}^{x}\sim\psi_{y}^{\dagger}\psi_{z}-\psi_{z}^{\dagger}\psi_{y}\), can be ignored due to the finite energy gap.

Since we have the NLsM description of all the fields, we can also re-express the Kondo couplings within the NLsM construction. Ignoring the gapped fields due to the static \(\langle Q^{3z^{2}-r^{2}}\rangle\) background in the \((\pi,\pi)\)-AFQ, we can write down the Kondo coupling Lagrangian as

\[\mathcal{L}_{K}= -J_{K}^{I}\left(2\ell_{2}^{z}+\frac{i}{2}\left(n_{A}^{y}-\bar{n}_{A }^{y}-n_{B}^{x}+\bar{n}_{B}^{x}\right)\right)s_{c}^{z}-J_{K}^{II}\left(2\ell_{1 }^{z}+\frac{1}{2}\left(n_{A}^{y}+\bar{n}_{A}^{y}+n_{B}^{x}+\bar{n}_{B}^{x} \right)\right)q_{c}^{xy}.\] (S75)We again integrate out the canting fields by utilizing the steepest descent approximation with

\[\frac{\delta\mathcal{L}}{\frac{\delta\mathcal{L}}{\delta\vartheta_{A}} =0;\quad\quad\frac{\delta\mathcal{L}}{\delta\nu}} =0,\] (S76) \[\frac{\delta\mathcal{L}}{\delta\vartheta_{A}} =0;\quad\frac{\delta\mathcal{L}}{\delta\vartheta_{B}} =0.\]

We then get the results

\[\ell_{1}^{z} =-\frac{\bar{n}_{A}\partial_{\tau}n_{B}-n_{A}\partial_{\tau}\bar{ n}_{B}}{16(J_{1}+J_{1}^{\prime})}+\frac{J_{K}^{II}}{16(J_{1}+J_{1}^{\prime})}q_{c} ^{xy},\] (S77) \[\ell_{2}^{z} =\frac{i(\bar{n}_{A}\partial_{\tau}n_{B}+n_{A}\partial_{\tau}\bar{ n}_{B})}{16(J_{1}-J_{2}^{\prime})}+\frac{J_{K}^{I}}{16(J_{1}-J_{2}^{\prime})}s_{c} ^{z},\] (S78) \[v_{A} =-\frac{i(\bar{n}_{C}\partial_{\tau}n_{A}+n_{C}\partial_{\tau} \bar{n}_{A})}{16J_{2}^{\prime}},\] (S79) \[v_{B} =-\frac{i(\bar{n}_{B}\partial_{\tau}n_{C}+n_{B}\partial_{\tau} \bar{n}_{C})}{16J_{2}^{\prime}}.\] (S80)

We can plug the solutions back to the Lagrangian to obtain

\[\mathcal{L}= \bar{n}_{A}\partial_{\tau}n_{A}+\bar{n}_{B}\partial_{\tau}n_{B}-\] \[-\frac{\left[\bar{n}_{A}\partial_{\tau}n_{B}-n_{A}\partial_{\tau }\bar{n}_{B}\right]^{2}}{16(J_{1}+J_{1}^{\prime})}+\frac{\left[\bar{n}_{A} \partial_{\tau}n_{B}+n_{A}\partial_{\tau}\bar{n}_{B}\right]^{2}}{16(J_{1}-J_{ 2}^{\prime})}-\frac{\left[\bar{n}_{C}\partial_{\tau}n_{A}+n_{C}\partial_{\tau }\bar{n}_{A}\right]}{32J_{2}^{\prime}}-\frac{\left[\bar{n}_{B}\partial_{\tau} n_{C}+n_{B}\partial_{\tau}\bar{n}_{C}\right]^{2}}{32J_{2}^{\prime}}+\] \[-J_{2}^{\prime}\sum_{\lambda}\left[\left(\partial_{\lambda}n_{A} \right)^{2}+\left(\partial_{\lambda}\bar{n}_{A}\right)^{2}+\left(\partial_{ \lambda}n_{B}\right)^{2}+\left(\partial_{\lambda}\bar{n}_{B}\right)^{2}\right] +4J_{1}^{\prime}\left|n_{A}\cdot n_{B}\right|^{2}-\] \[-J_{K}^{II}\left[\frac{i}{2}\left(n_{A}^{y}-\bar{n}_{A}^{y}-n_{B}^ {x}+\bar{n}_{B}^{x}\right)+\frac{i\left(\bar{n}_{A}\partial_{\tau}n_{B}+n_{A} \partial_{\tau}n_{B}\right)}{8(J_{1}-J_{2}^{\prime})}\right]s_{c}^{z}\] \[-J_{K}^{II}\left[\frac{1}{2}\left(n_{A}^{y}+\bar{n}_{A}^{y}+n_{B} ^{x}+\bar{n}_{B}^{x}\right)-\frac{\bar{n}_{A}\partial_{\tau}n_{B}-n_{A} \partial_{\tau}\bar{n}_{B}}{8(J_{1}+J_{1}^{\prime})}\right]q_{c}^{xy}-\] \[-\frac{(J_{K}^{I})^{2}}{16(J_{1}-J_{2}^{\prime})}(s_{c}^{z})^{2}- \frac{(J_{K}^{II})^{2}}{16(J_{1}+J_{1}^{\prime})}(q_{c}^{xy})^{2},\] (S81)

where we explicitly suppress the constant static background \(Q^{3z^{2}-r^{2}}\) field which breaks SU(3) symmetry between three flavored conduction electrons. Focusing on the quadrupolar order, we again use the identity \(\bar{n}_{a}\partial_{\tau}n_{a}=1/2\partial_{\tau}(n_{a})^{2}\simeq 0\), and can be ignored.

Re-express the fields in the bosonic fields using Eq. S63, the Lagrangian becomes

\[\mathcal{L}\simeq \frac{(\partial_{\tau}\phi_{1})^{2}}{4(J_{1}+J_{2}-K_{2})}+2(K_{1} -2K_{2})\sum_{\lambda}(\partial_{\lambda}\phi_{1})^{2}+\sum_{a=2,3}\left[ \frac{(\partial_{\tau}\phi_{a})^{2}}{8(J_{2}-K_{2})}-2K_{2}\sum_{\lambda}( \partial_{\lambda}\phi_{a})^{2}\right]+\] \[+\frac{(\partial_{\tau}\phi_{4})^{2}}{4K_{1}}+2(K_{1}+2K_{2}-4J_{ 2})\sum_{\lambda}(\partial_{\lambda}\phi_{4})^{2}+16(K_{1}-J_{1})\phi_{4}^{2}-\] \[-\frac{iJ_{K}^{I}}{4(J_{1}+J_{2}-K_{2})}s_{c}^{z}(\partial_{\tau} \phi_{1})+\frac{iJ_{K}^{II}}{4K_{1}}q_{c}^{xy}(\partial_{\tau}\phi_{4})-\frac{( J_{K}^{II})^{2}}{16K_{1}}(q_{c}^{xy})^{2}-\frac{(J_{K}^{I})^{2}}{16(J_{1}+J_{2}-K_{2})}(s_{ c}^{z})^{2}\] \[\equiv\mathcal{L}_{S}^{NLsM}(\phi_{1},\phi_{2},\phi_{3},\phi_{4})+ \mathcal{L}_{K}+\mathcal{L}(\psi^{4}),\] (S82)

where the last two terms \(\mathcal{L}(\psi^{4})\) only renormalize the Fermi velocity and the quartic fermion terms and can be ignored. Focusing on the point away from the SU(3) point, \(K_{1}>J_{1}\), we can also ignore \(\phi_{4}\) due to the finite mass term. In the end we conclude the NLsM for the \((\pi,\pi)\)-AFQ in the presence of Kondo couplings to the 3-flavor conduction electrons is

\[S_{(\pi,\pi)-AFQ}^{NLsM}\simeq\int_{0}^{\beta}\frac{d\tau}{2}\int d^{2}r\Bigg{\{} \frac{(\partial_{\tau}\phi_{1})^{2}}{4(J_{1}+J_{2}-K_{2})}+2(K_{1}-2K_{2})\sum_{ \lambda}(\partial_{\lambda}\phi_{1})^{2}+\sum_{a=2,3}\left[\frac{(\partial_{ \tau}\phi_{a})^{2}}{8(J_{2}-K_{2})}-2K_{2}\sum_{\lambda}(\partial_{\lambda}\phi _{a})^{2}\right]\Bigg{\}},\] (S83)

\[S_{K}\simeq\int_{0}^{\beta}\frac{d\tau}{2}\int d^{2}r\left(\lambda _{z}s_{c}^{z}\partial_{\tau}\phi_{1}\right),\] (S84) \[S_{c}\simeq\int d^{d}\mathbf{K}d\epsilon\sum_{\alpha=x,y}\psi_{ \alpha}^{\dagger}(\mathbf{K},i\epsilon)(i\epsilon-\xi_{K})\psi_{\alpha}(\mathbf{K},i \epsilon)+\int d^{d}\mathbf{K}^{\prime}d\epsilon^{\prime}\psi_{z}^{\dagger}(\mathbf{K} ^{\prime},i\epsilon^{\prime})(i\epsilon^{\prime}-\tilde{\xi}_{K^{\prime}}) \psi_{z}(\mathbf{K}^{\prime},i\epsilon^{\prime}),\] (S85)

where we define \(\lambda_{z}=-iJ_{K}^{I}/[4(J_{1}+J_{2}-K_{2})]\), \(\xi_{K}=v_{F}(K-K_{F})\), and \(\tilde{\xi}_{K^{\prime}}=\tilde{v}_{F}(K^{\prime}-\tilde{K}_{F}^{\prime})\).

## X Exact marginality of Kondo couplings

In this work, we follow the scaling procedure illustrated in Ref. [S6] to conclude the exact marginality of the Kondo coupling \(\lambda_{z}\) in the \((\pi,\pi)\)-AFQ. For clarity in the scaling analysis, we first define \(\Phi_{a}(\mathbf{r},\tau)\equiv\partial_{\tau}\phi_{a}(\mathbf{r},\tau)\). The scaling dimension of \(\Phi_{a}(\mathbf{r},\tau)\) can be directly read out, \(\Delta[\Phi_{a}(\mathbf{r},\tau)]=1\), which leads to the scaling dimension of its Fourier partner as \(\Delta[\Phi_{a}(\mathbf{q},\omega)]=-d\), where \(d\) is the spatial dimension. For the conduction electron fields, the scaling dimension of a servonic field is \(\Delta[\psi_{c}(\mathbf{K},\omega)]=-3/2\). We can see that at tree level, the scaling dimension of the Kondo coupling is _marginal_,

\[\Delta[S_{K}]\Big{|}_{tree-level}=\Delta[dkd\epsilon d^{d}qd\omega\psi_{c}^{x \dagger}(\mathbf{k}+\mathbf{q},\epsilon+\omega)\psi_{c}^{y}(\mathbf{k},\omega)\Phi_{1}( \mathbf{q},\omega)]=1+1+d+1+2(-3/2)-d=0.\] (S86)

Now we will show that the result at tree level is exact based on procedure in Ref. [S6].

Considering a spherical Fermi surface of conduction electrons, we first re-express the momentum integral in the part of the action of the conduction electron as the momentum integral near the Fermi surface. Keeping the most relevant term, we obtain

\[\int d^{d}\mathbf{K}=\int_{K_{F}-\Lambda}^{K_{F}+\Lambda}K^{d-1}dK\int d^{d-1} \Omega_{K}\simeq K_{F}^{d-1}\int_{-\Lambda}^{\Lambda}dk\int d^{d-1}\Omega_{K}\] (S87)

, where we introduce \(k=K_{F}-K\) and keep only the \(K_{F}^{d-1}\) terms after Taylor expansion. Now the kinetic part of the fermions can be re-expressed as

\[S_{c}\simeq K_{F}^{d-1}\int dk_{\alpha}d^{d-1}\Omega_{K}d\epsilon\psi_{c}^{ \alpha\dagger}\left(i\epsilon_{\alpha}-v_{F}^{\alpha}k_{\alpha}\right)\psi_{c}^ {\alpha}=\int d\bar{k}_{\alpha}d^{d-1}\bar{\Omega}_{K}d\bar{v}_{c}^{\alpha \dagger}\left(\bar{\epsilon}_{\alpha}-v_{F}^{\alpha}\bar{k}_{\alpha}\right) \bar{\psi}_{c}^{\alpha},\] (S88)

where we introduce the dimensionless couplings, \(\epsilon=\Lambda\bar{\epsilon}\), \(k=\Lambda\bar{k}\), \(\Omega_{K}=\bar{\Omega}_{K}\), \(K_{F}^{d-1}\Lambda^{3}\psi^{\dagger}\psi=\bar{\psi}^{\dagger}\bar{\psi}\). For the action of the bosonic fields, Eq. (S83), we perform similar transformation, \(\int d^{2}\mathbf{q}d\omega(\mathbf{q}^{2}+\omega^{2})\phi_{1}^{2}(\mathbf{q},\omega)=\int d ^{2}\mathbf{q}d\bar{\omega}(\bar{\mathbf{q}}^{2}+\bar{\omega}^{2})\bar{\phi}_{1}^{2}( \bar{\mathbf{q}},\bar{\omega})\), where we define \(\Lambda^{5}\phi_{1}^{2}=\bar{\phi}_{1}^{2}\). Plugging the new definition into the Kondo action, we find that at \(d=2\) it takes the form \(\sim\int dkd\epsilon d^{2}qd\omega\psi_{c}^{x\dagger}(k+q,\epsilon+\omega)\psi_ {c}^{y}(k,\omega)\omega\phi_{1}(q,\omega)\), which leads to

\[\frac{S_{K}}{S_{c}}\propto\Lambda^{1+1+2+1}\Lambda^{-3}K_{F}^{1-2}\Lambda^{1- \frac{5}{2}}=\frac{\sqrt{\Lambda}}{K_{F}},\] (S89)

where we can see in the limit \(\Lambda/K_{F}\to 0\), _i.e._, the Fermi momentum is much larger than the thin-shell momentum cut-off near the Fermi surface, the Kondo coupling is heavily suppressed.Therefore, the Kondo vertex is associated with positive powers of \(\sqrt{\Lambda}/K_{F}\) which is vanishingly small. As the number of powers of Kondo couplings increases, so does the suppression factor, and, therefore, all higher-order terms are suppressed, which means that the scaling result at tree-level RG analysis is exact. The Kondo coupling is indeed exactly _marginal_.

